From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jaime Dias <jaimejosediasnt@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 7 Aug 2026 08:30:00 -0300
Subject: [PATCH 09/22] exec: name the caller that frees outside the pool

patches/aros/0010 refuses a free whose pointer is outside the allocator's
areas, which stops the corruption but says nothing about who is responsible.
By the time TLSF notices, the code that passed the pointer has returned. The
open question in ISSUE-0007 has been "who", for days, and every attempt to
answer it from the guest's A7 was retracted.

Answer it one frame earlier instead. FreeVecPooled is the only door into
mhe_FreeVec from outside the allocator, and it is a library entry point
reached through a jump table, so __builtin_return_address(0) is the caller
itself -- not a trampoline.

That level matters on m68k. The intended instrument was the frame-pointer
walk upstream calls from tlsf.c (KrnBacktraceFromFrame, added 2026-08-02),
but there is no m68k implementation of it and rom/kernel/backtracefromframe.c
returns 0. Writing one would not help either: the kernel builds with -Os and
carries no frames at all -- 23 functions in tlsf.o, not one linkw %fp. Level
0 needs no frame, and here it is enough.

The test is the allocator's own: mhe_InBounds, which TLSF implements as
tlsf_in_bounds() by walking its area list -- the same walk 0010 does. So this
asks rather than duplicates, and a pool with no InBounds is left alone.

Costs one indirect call per FreeVecPooled and prints only when a free is
refused.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 rom/exec/freevecpooled.c | 32 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 32 insertions(+)

diff --git a/rom/exec/freevecpooled.c b/rom/exec/freevecpooled.c
index efe029d506..e214f456ee 100644
--- a/rom/exec/freevecpooled.c
+++ b/rom/exec/freevecpooled.c
@@ -2,6 +2,7 @@
     Copyright (C) 2003-2013, The AROS Development Team. All rights reserved.
 */
 
+#include <aros/debug.h>
 #include <aros/libcall.h>
 #include <proto/exec.h>
 #include <exec/memheaderext.h>
@@ -58,6 +59,37 @@
 
     if (IsManagedMem(mhe))
     {
+        /*
+         * Name the caller before handing the pointer to the allocator.
+         *
+         * A bad free is noticed inside the allocator, long after whoever
+         * passed the pointer has returned, and the block address on its own
+         * says nothing about who is responsible. Here the caller is still
+         * one frame away: this is a library entry point reached through a
+         * jump table, so __builtin_return_address(0) is the caller itself.
+         * That matters on m68k, where the build omits frame pointers and
+         * there is no walk to fall back on -- level 0 is all there is, and
+         * it is enough because this is the only door into mhe_FreeVec.
+         *
+         * mhe_InBounds is the allocator's own answer, so this asks rather
+         * than assumes: TLSF walks its area list (tlsf_in_bounds), which is
+         * the same check that refusing the free inside tlsf_freevec makes.
+         */
+        if (mhe->mhe_InBounds && !mhe->mhe_InBounds(mhe, memory, memory))
+        {
+            struct Task *task = SysBase->ThisTask;
+
+            bug("[FreeVecPooled] REFUSED %p: not in pool %p '%s'"
+                " -- caller %p, task %p '%s'\n",
+                memory, mhe,
+                mhe->mhe_MemHeader.mh_Node.ln_Name ?
+                    mhe->mhe_MemHeader.mh_Node.ln_Name : "<unnamed>",
+                __builtin_return_address(0), task,
+                (task && task->tc_Node.ln_Name) ?
+                    task->tc_Node.ln_Name : "<unnamed>");
+            return;
+        }
+
         if (mhe->mhe_FreeVec)
             mhe->mhe_FreeVec(mhe, memory);
     }
